WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:03.790
This series takes a closer look at each part of HASTE.

00:04.220 --> 00:07.840
In this episode: the standard labeling workflow, where you

00:07.860 --> 00:09.640
train a custom damage model from

00:09.660 --> 00:11.430
a few dozen hand-drawn labels.

00:15.300 --> 00:18.910
We start in the Spokane project, and add a new image layer.

00:19.907 --> 00:22.957
This time, we choose the standard labeling workflow.

00:24.213 --> 00:26.123
Here, we're using Planet data:

00:26.513 --> 00:28.963
SkySat post-event imagery from August 4th.

00:29.747 --> 00:31.827
Then we add pre-event Bing Maps aerial

00:31.847 --> 00:33.677
imagery, and building footprints.

00:38.433 --> 00:40.903
HASTE prepares the imagery in the background.

00:41.353 --> 00:43.783
When it's ready, we launch the labeling tool.

00:51.433 --> 00:55.213
The imagery panel adjusts contrast and color, and toggles

00:55.233 --> 00:59.343
between pre- and post-event imagery, so damage is easy to see.

01:00.400 --> 01:02.990
Labels are polygons, each with a class.

01:03.360 --> 01:06.050
We pick Damaged Building, and outline burned homes.

01:17.633 --> 01:19.643
Then intact homes, as Building.

01:23.467 --> 01:25.487
And Background, for everything that isn't

01:25.507 --> 01:28.237
a building: yards, trees, and the street.

01:30.033 --> 01:32.493
Then we work across the whole scene, and zoom

01:32.513 --> 01:35.243
out for forest, the river, and open ground.

01:35.873 --> 01:37.433
Diverse examples teach the model

01:37.453 --> 01:39.463
what is, and isn't, a building.

01:40.467 --> 01:43.237
Burned homes look different under tree cover,

01:43.367 --> 01:46.157
so we add examples from a second neighborhood, too.

01:59.967 --> 02:03.577
And intact homes nearby, so the model learns the difference.

02:16.700 --> 02:19.450
Ninety-two labels are enough to get started.

02:21.000 --> 02:23.890
Save and Train opens the training settings.

02:24.240 --> 02:26.680
We train for 10 epochs, at a learning rate of

02:26.700 --> 02:31.600
0.001. HASTE fine-tunes a U-Net segmentation

02:31.620 --> 02:34.580
model, with a ResNeXt-50 encoder pre-trained

02:34.600 --> 02:37.010
on ImageNet, on this imagery alone.

02:40.533 --> 02:43.453
About 18 minutes later, the model is trained,

02:43.473 --> 02:46.263
and inference has run across the whole scene.

02:50.967 --> 02:54.167
The results viewer colors every building footprint by the

02:54.187 --> 02:58.177
model's prediction: red for damaged, and green for intact.

02:59.937 --> 03:01.757
The raw model output shows the

03:01.777 --> 03:03.847
per-pixel segmentation behind it,

03:08.957 --> 03:11.827
and we can swipe to pre-event imagery to check its work.

03:15.700 --> 03:17.490
Back on the project page,

03:17.640 --> 03:20.000
download the scored buildings as a GeoPackage,

03:20.020 --> 03:22.370
or the training and inference artifacts,

03:22.560 --> 03:24.110
including the model itself.

03:24.840 --> 03:27.150
Later in the series, we'll edit these predictions.

03:28.500 --> 03:31.870
HASTE is open source under the MIT license.

03:32.340 --> 03:35.450
Deploy it to Azure with a single azd up command,

03:35.540 --> 03:37.750
or run it locally with Docker Compose.

03:38.200 --> 03:41.130
Find it on GitHub at microsoft/haste.
